WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.415 --> 00:00:02.677
- Not one drop of my self worth depends

00:00:02.677 --> 00:00:04.391
on your acceptance of me.

00:00:04.391 --> 00:00:07.317
It's work when they say that
the dictionary's the only place

00:00:07.317 --> 00:00:10.285
you see success before work
and that's alphabetical.

00:00:10.285 --> 00:00:11.452
I was ready to

00:00:12.749 --> 00:00:13.855
do myself in.

00:00:13.855 --> 00:00:15.566
I just couldn't handle
the pressure anymore.

00:00:15.566 --> 00:00:18.953
From day one, we need how
to play every kind of music.

00:00:18.953 --> 00:00:22.360
Our entire business is
all based on two things,

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.815
a great song and a great story.

00:00:24.815 --> 00:00:27.578
- He's an American record
producer, composer, musician,

00:00:27.578 --> 00:00:29.493
and entertainment executive.

00:00:29.493 --> 00:00:32.391
His career spans six decades
in the entertainment industry

00:00:32.391 --> 00:00:36.428
and he's been nominated for
a record 79 Grammy awards.

00:00:36.428 --> 00:00:38.428
Time magazine listed him as one

00:00:38.428 --> 00:00:41.215
of the most influential jazz
musicians of the 20th Century.

00:00:41.215 --> 00:00:43.672
He's Quincy Jones and here's
my take on his top ten rules

00:00:43.672 --> 00:00:44.696
for success.

00:00:44.696 --> 00:00:46.618
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:46.618 --> 00:00:48.198
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:48.198 --> 00:00:50.615
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:56.487
- Number one is to know who
you are and learning to love

00:00:56.487 --> 00:00:57.487
who you are.

00:00:58.718 --> 00:01:00.128
'Cause you can't love anybody else

00:01:00.128 --> 00:01:01.634
if you don't love yourself.

00:01:01.634 --> 00:01:04.628
Ray Charles and I used to
say, every day in Seattle,

00:01:04.628 --> 00:01:07.985
not one drop of my self worth
depends on your acceptance

00:01:07.985 --> 00:01:09.259
of me.

00:01:09.259 --> 00:01:11.846
Every day, that was the deal
with racism and everything,

00:01:11.846 --> 00:01:14.797
because we don't want an
external force defining

00:01:14.797 --> 00:01:16.485
who the hell you are.

00:01:16.485 --> 00:01:18.524
- [Interviewer] Where
have you gone, to improve

00:01:18.524 --> 00:01:21.074
your arranging skills, as an arranger?

00:01:21.074 --> 00:01:24.117
- (laughs) work and study, man.

00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:25.372
Work and study.

00:01:25.372 --> 00:01:27.919
I did that a long time ago.

00:01:27.919 --> 00:01:30.874
I put 28 years in my core skill

00:01:30.874 --> 00:01:35.070
and orchestration,
counter point, everything.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:39.237
Music is always left and right
brain, intellect and emotion,

00:01:40.138 --> 00:01:42.595
and you've got to work to
keep the science going.

00:01:42.595 --> 00:01:44.315
You don't work to keep the science going,

00:01:44.315 --> 00:01:45.904
you can't support the emotion.

00:01:45.904 --> 00:01:47.393
A million guys come and tell me,

00:01:47.393 --> 00:01:48.903
"man, if I could play piano,

00:01:48.903 --> 00:01:50.923
"I could be better than Herby Hancock."

00:01:50.923 --> 00:01:52.859
I say, yeah, but you
ain't going to be better

00:01:52.859 --> 00:01:55.311
than Herby Hancock because
you haven't worked.

00:01:55.311 --> 00:01:56.398
But you have to work.

00:01:56.398 --> 00:01:57.396
It's work man.

00:01:57.396 --> 00:01:59.977
They say that the dictionary's
the only place you see

00:01:59.977 --> 00:02:02.845
success before work and
that's alphabetical.

00:02:02.845 --> 00:02:04.345
In school, we were

00:02:05.487 --> 00:02:08.951
exposed to march music and classical music

00:02:08.951 --> 00:02:11.034
and semi-classical music.

00:02:12.818 --> 00:02:15.332
To make a living, you
had to play schottische

00:02:15.332 --> 00:02:19.895
and all those things, so it
was always the full range

00:02:19.895 --> 00:02:21.750
of music around.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.236
I remember once Ray Charles said to me,

00:02:25.236 --> 00:02:28.111
we were trying to put a bee-bop feeling

00:02:28.111 --> 00:02:29.500
in the schottische one time

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.000
and he says, "no, you just
put your soul into the music

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.583
"you're playing and
let it be a schottische

00:02:36.583 --> 00:02:37.804
and it'll be okay."

00:02:37.804 --> 00:02:39.355
I never forgot that.

00:02:39.355 --> 00:02:43.156
- When Quincy Jones was
stranded in Europe, he realized

00:02:43.156 --> 00:02:46.636
the importance of being a
good business man as well

00:02:46.636 --> 00:02:48.719
as being a good musician.

00:02:49.624 --> 00:02:53.029
He had to really look at
every aspect of the business

00:02:53.029 --> 00:02:55.202
to try to get home.

00:02:55.202 --> 00:02:56.404
This is my responsibility.

00:02:56.404 --> 00:02:58.369
I got him over here, I can get him back.

00:02:58.369 --> 00:02:59.702
- I was ready to

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:01.947
do myself in.

00:03:01.947 --> 00:03:03.782
I just couldn't handle
the pressure anymore

00:03:03.782 --> 00:03:05.923
because it was performing,
and the payrolls,

00:03:05.923 --> 00:03:08.020
and the book and everything else.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:12.022
I think when people think
about taking their life,

00:03:12.022 --> 00:03:15.437
it's just about 15 minutes peace,

00:03:15.437 --> 00:03:18.714
just to get some rest for awhile.

00:03:18.714 --> 00:03:21.464
It's amazing how it works because

00:03:24.046 --> 00:03:26.945
a whole series of mistakes, at one point,

00:03:26.945 --> 00:03:29.009
people start to call experience.

00:03:29.009 --> 00:03:31.234
Oh, he's very experienced in that.

00:03:31.234 --> 00:03:33.117
That means you've had a
chance to mess up a lot.

00:03:33.117 --> 00:03:35.143
That's great, because if
you don't take any chances,

00:03:35.143 --> 00:03:37.817
you're not going to make
any mistakes, you know.

00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:41.613
My father said to us
every day of our lives,

00:03:41.613 --> 00:03:45.242
"once a task is just begun,
never leave it 'til it's done,

00:03:45.242 --> 00:03:49.363
"be the labor great or small,
do it well or not at all."

00:03:49.363 --> 00:03:52.314
Every day of our lives he said that.

00:03:52.314 --> 00:03:54.789
He would never let us half step.

00:03:54.789 --> 00:03:57.062
That I learned from him
and I treasure that though,

00:03:57.062 --> 00:04:00.418
'cause he didn't believe
in doing anything half way.

00:04:00.418 --> 00:04:02.261
- What advice do you have for the future

00:04:02.261 --> 00:04:05.566
of making great records as a producer?

00:04:05.566 --> 00:04:07.701
- Know what you're doing.

00:04:07.701 --> 00:04:09.855
Study everything.

00:04:09.855 --> 00:04:10.855
Study music.

00:04:12.284 --> 00:04:15.591
I'm hearing some funny stuff
now that drives me crazy.

00:04:15.591 --> 00:04:18.300
I'll talk to some of the producers today.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.499
We were doing an amazing project

00:04:21.499 --> 00:04:23.698
in Morocco last year.

00:04:23.698 --> 00:04:27.365
We had all of the number one
singers in the Arab Spring,

00:04:27.365 --> 00:04:32.331
Libya, Egypt, Jordan,
Aleppo, Damascus, everywhere.

00:04:32.331 --> 00:04:35.582
We were recording over there
and I'm calling on names

00:04:35.582 --> 00:04:38.350
over there and I said,
"do you read music?"

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.807
He said, "yeah, I used to, but I forgot."

00:04:40.807 --> 00:04:44.752
Give me a break man, you don't
forget how to read music.

00:04:44.752 --> 00:04:47.640
Man, you have to understand,
to put the work in

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.452
to learn the science, man.

00:04:49.452 --> 00:04:50.285
You do.

00:04:50.285 --> 00:04:52.828
You can't play a trumpet
if you don't know how to--

00:04:52.828 --> 00:04:55.912
it's a science, the armistice,
the diaphragmatic breathing,

00:04:55.912 --> 00:05:00.335
there's a lot of secrets with
everything you attempt to do.

00:05:00.335 --> 00:05:04.128
Retrograde, inverting, counter point.

00:05:04.128 --> 00:05:06.790
There's only 12 notes,
man, so you can figure out

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:09.435
what you have to do to get your own sound.

00:05:09.435 --> 00:05:13.602
You have to do that through
rhythm, melody and harmony,

00:05:14.513 --> 00:05:17.562
and you have to do a lot of
research to figure that out.

00:05:17.562 --> 00:05:19.688
You have to know what's going on.

00:05:19.688 --> 00:05:24.369
You have to understand, I
don't know a Bravelle and

00:05:24.369 --> 00:05:28.511
Oldenburg and Basie
and Duke and Bo Diddley

00:05:28.511 --> 00:05:32.303
and Melle Mel and Ludacris
and Drake and all of them.

00:05:32.303 --> 00:05:35.917
Come on man, it's doing
your homework, man.

00:05:35.917 --> 00:05:37.191
That's all I can say.

00:05:37.191 --> 00:05:41.401
Prepare yourself, 'cause my
thing was, my biggest fear,

00:05:41.401 --> 00:05:44.035
and I don't play with that word at all,

00:05:44.035 --> 00:05:47.782
was to get great opportunity
that I'm not prepared for.

00:05:47.782 --> 00:05:51.678
When Frank Sinatra called
me, man, I was ready for him.

00:05:51.678 --> 00:05:52.759
I'm telling you.

00:05:52.759 --> 00:05:53.936
And he tested me.

00:05:53.936 --> 00:05:55.670
I'm telling you, Ray Charles
too, 'cause those guys

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:56.503
are rough man.

00:05:56.503 --> 00:05:58.791
Billy Eckstine, Ray
Charles, Frank Sinatra,

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:00.958
they will kick your booty.

00:06:02.286 --> 00:06:05.220
He'd say to me, we were
doing an arrangement

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:09.326
at Capital Studios, he'd
say, "Q, that first 8

00:06:09.326 --> 00:06:10.189
"was a little dense."

00:06:10.189 --> 00:06:12.144
So I say, no problem man
and I put all the horns,

00:06:12.144 --> 00:06:14.707
the trumpets and the (mumbles) out,

00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:17.024
transfer the saxophones
to the alto flutes,

00:06:17.024 --> 00:06:18.587
stuff like, let's go.

00:06:18.587 --> 00:06:19.604
He was testing me.

00:06:19.604 --> 00:06:21.678
He only did it three times
and after that, he said,

00:06:21.678 --> 00:06:23.370
"he knows what he's doing."

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:26.610
I was ready for it and
that's the biggest compliment

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:29.632
and accomplishment you
can have in your life,

00:06:29.632 --> 00:06:31.948
is to say, I've go my stuff together.

00:06:31.948 --> 00:06:34.955
I got my (bleep) together.

00:06:34.955 --> 00:06:36.401
You have to have it together man.

00:06:36.401 --> 00:06:37.480
(audience applauding)

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:38.313
You do.

00:06:38.313 --> 00:06:40.687
- Let me ask you at creativity
then, and the diversity

00:06:40.687 --> 00:06:43.522
that is Quincy Jones, because
I'm a kid of the 1980s,

00:06:43.522 --> 00:06:46.368
so I know Quincy Jones, the
famous guy, the hit maker,

00:06:46.368 --> 00:06:49.472
the pop wizard, the R&amp;B soul guy.

00:06:49.472 --> 00:06:51.954
You were a serious jazz
guy through the 50s.

00:06:51.954 --> 00:06:53.752
I mean, you were on tour
with Lionel Hampton--

00:06:53.752 --> 00:06:57.527
- No, no, no, understand
man, from day one,

00:06:57.527 --> 00:06:59.701
we knew how to play every kind of music,

00:06:59.701 --> 00:07:04.151
Sousa, Debussy, Schottische,
for Bar-mitzvahs,

00:07:04.151 --> 00:07:06.153
rhythm and blues, everything.

00:07:06.153 --> 00:07:07.459
We played everything, man.

00:07:07.459 --> 00:07:10.937
I got a lot of, this is when I started

00:07:10.937 --> 00:07:12.149
doing Michael Jackson.

00:07:12.149 --> 00:07:14.085
I didn't have to learn
anything to do Michael.

00:07:14.085 --> 00:07:16.076
I mean, I've been doing that all my life.

00:07:16.076 --> 00:07:18.409
- That's my question though.

00:07:20.074 --> 00:07:22.809
Is the guy who toured with
Count Basie or did arrangements

00:07:22.809 --> 00:07:26.088
for Count Basie or Gene
Krupa, Sarah Vaughan--

00:07:26.088 --> 00:07:26.945
- Everybody.

00:07:26.945 --> 00:07:30.754
- That's the same guy who can
produce hip hop or pop music?

00:07:30.754 --> 00:07:34.809
- Bubba, we were doing it all our lives.

00:07:34.809 --> 00:07:38.490
Nadia Boulanger said,
"Quincy, God gave us 12 notes,

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:43.341
"the Bach, Brahams,
Beethoven, Bo Diddley, Basie,

00:07:43.341 --> 00:07:45.342
"Bird, all use the same 12 notes."

00:07:45.342 --> 00:07:47.848
"Until he gives us 13, I want
you to know what everybody

00:07:47.848 --> 00:07:49.373
"did with 12."

00:07:49.373 --> 00:07:52.123
I studied hard for 28 years, man,

00:07:54.227 --> 00:07:56.732
and for seven decades, we
never stopped, you know.

00:07:56.732 --> 00:07:58.690
We tried to play like Miles, tried to play

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:01.247
like Charlie Parker, like (mumbles)

00:08:01.247 --> 00:08:04.748
and when you come out of that,
there's 10 different people

00:08:04.748 --> 00:08:08.176
and your personality is going to finally,

00:08:08.176 --> 00:08:11.313
not transcend that but morph

00:08:11.313 --> 00:08:15.325
into its own personality
because it is a personality.

00:08:15.325 --> 00:08:19.370
If you have traveled the
roads of all the great people,

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:21.058
but I think that happens with anything.

00:08:21.058 --> 00:08:24.297
If it's a camera man,
you know, you look at

00:08:24.297 --> 00:08:27.805
(mumbles), whatever,
all your favorite people

00:08:27.805 --> 00:08:29.880
and just deal with just the best.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.778
Or like classical composers
or whatever and people

00:08:32.778 --> 00:08:35.675
that deal with drama, you know
with the elements of drama,

00:08:35.675 --> 00:08:38.258
they all do it a different way.

00:08:39.126 --> 00:08:40.510
Jump into it.

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:41.602
Jump all over it.

00:08:41.602 --> 00:08:42.505
I'm serious.

00:08:42.505 --> 00:08:44.250
Don't be bashful.

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:45.439
Jump into it.

00:08:45.439 --> 00:08:47.704
Try to copy it, just like it is.

00:08:47.704 --> 00:08:49.685
You can't do this on a professional level,

00:08:49.685 --> 00:08:53.108
I mean in training your
mind to think on that level,

00:08:53.108 --> 00:08:55.837
the highest level, go with
everybody from Johnny Williams

00:08:55.837 --> 00:08:58.420
to Goldsmith to Alan Sylvestre,

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.118
Stanley Clark, everybody.

00:09:02.118 --> 00:09:03.511
I have seven kids.

00:09:03.511 --> 00:09:06.154
I have six daughters, 21 to 16,

00:09:06.154 --> 00:09:07.728
that's why I don't have any hair.

00:09:07.728 --> 00:09:09.977
(audience laughing)

00:09:09.977 --> 00:09:10.977
And one son,

00:09:13.103 --> 00:09:14.554
but my part of the tough love

00:09:14.554 --> 00:09:16.562
is to tell them, the first thing they have

00:09:16.562 --> 00:09:18.243
to know is know who you are.

00:09:18.243 --> 00:09:21.005
Because a lot of people
don't know who they are.

00:09:21.005 --> 00:09:23.547
And that's very difficult, but
that's the first challenge,

00:09:23.547 --> 00:09:25.315
I think, is to know who you are.

00:09:25.315 --> 00:09:27.317
If it's 70/30, work on it.

00:09:27.317 --> 00:09:30.689
At the 30%, stop whining and move on.

00:09:30.689 --> 00:09:34.147
Then, after you know who
you are, love who you are.

00:09:34.147 --> 00:09:35.684
That's the first two steps.

00:09:35.684 --> 00:09:37.211
(audience applauding)

00:09:37.211 --> 00:09:39.044
Mantra to my children.

00:09:40.822 --> 00:09:43.404
'Cause you're on your way
once you know who you are

00:09:43.404 --> 00:09:45.132
and love who you are.

00:09:45.132 --> 00:09:47.474
You're on the way, because if
you don't love who you are,

00:09:47.474 --> 00:09:48.922
you're in trouble.

00:09:48.922 --> 00:09:51.120
Always be true to yourself, baby.

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.453
Our entire business is
all based on two things,

00:09:54.453 --> 00:09:57.409
a great song and a great story.

00:09:57.409 --> 00:10:00.235
Film, television, if you
don't have that story,

00:10:00.235 --> 00:10:01.687
nothing else matters.

00:10:01.687 --> 00:10:04.410
You don't call anybody
else, or direct anybody.

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:06.930
The same with a song, great song can make

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.680
the worst singer in the world a star.

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:12.726
But a bad song, the three
best singers in the world

00:10:12.726 --> 00:10:14.059
can not save it.

00:10:14.918 --> 00:10:16.801
And that's the bottom line man.

00:10:16.801 --> 00:10:19.796
- [Male] If you are not
working as a musician

00:10:19.796 --> 00:10:21.471
until the later years of your life--

00:10:21.471 --> 00:10:22.625
- It doesn't matter man.

00:10:22.625 --> 00:10:24.377
At 60 you can start, man.

00:10:24.377 --> 00:10:26.271
Nadia Boulanger told me that.

00:10:26.271 --> 00:10:29.123
She said, "you can become an
attorney at 60 years old."

00:10:29.123 --> 00:10:31.050
Man, you do whatever you feel like doing,

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:33.280
if you're willing to put
your mindset behind it,

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:36.074
the passion, and the work.

00:10:36.074 --> 00:10:37.738
Anything, anytime in life.

00:10:37.738 --> 00:10:38.918
(audience applauding)

00:10:38.918 --> 00:10:39.874
70 years old.

00:10:39.874 --> 00:10:44.041
I mean, I feel like I'm just
starting man, I really do.

00:10:45.444 --> 00:10:46.945
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:10:46.945 --> 00:10:49.680
I made this video because
Ari Gabriel asked me to.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:51.847
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want

00:10:51.847 --> 00:10:53.620
me to profile next, leave
it in the comments below

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:54.890
and I'll see what I can do.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:56.852
I also would love to know
which of Quincy's top ten rules

00:10:56.852 --> 00:10:58.670
had the biggest impact on you and why.

00:10:58.670 --> 00:10:59.862
Leave it in the comments.

00:10:59.862 --> 00:11:00.813
I will join in the discussion.

00:11:00.813 --> 00:11:03.489
Lastly, wanted to give a
quick shout out to Mark Drager

00:11:03.489 --> 00:11:04.551
from Phanta Media.

00:11:04.551 --> 00:11:06.890
Mark, thank you so much
for buying one of my books.

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:08.058
It means a lot to me.

00:11:08.058 --> 00:11:10.090
For those of you watching,
if you want a chance

00:11:10.090 --> 00:11:12.004
at a shout out in a future
video, make sure to pick up

00:11:12.004 --> 00:11:13.398
a copy of the book and
email in your receipt

00:11:13.398 --> 00:11:14.562
so we can keep track.

00:11:14.562 --> 00:11:15.739
Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:15.739 --> 00:11:18.273
Continue to believe, or
whatever your one word is

00:11:18.273 --> 00:11:20.106
and I'll see you soon.

00:11:25.846 --> 00:11:29.062
- It starts with a human being you know.

00:11:29.062 --> 00:11:31.172
Nadia Boulanger said that to me every day.

00:11:31.172 --> 00:11:33.548
"Quincy, your music can never
be more or less than you are

00:11:33.548 --> 00:11:35.037
"as a human being."

00:11:35.037 --> 00:11:36.675
That's what it's about.

00:11:36.675 --> 00:11:38.692
You can have all that
science and technique

00:11:38.692 --> 00:11:39.990
and everything else.

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:42.348
If you don't have a life
and love and live and give

00:11:42.348 --> 00:11:45.039
and stuff like that, you
have nothing to say, you know

00:11:45.039 --> 00:11:48.013
to share, you have
nothing to share, to give,

00:11:48.013 --> 00:11:49.463
and she's right, you know.

00:11:49.463 --> 00:11:51.071
Man, I have lived man.

00:11:51.071 --> 00:11:52.238
I did my best.

00:11:53.866 --> 00:11:55.976
After Ray Charles, are you kidding?

00:11:55.976 --> 00:11:57.059
Basie, Miles,

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:01.294
Sinatra, please.

00:12:01.294 --> 00:12:05.415
Sinatra used to tell us every
night we got off the stand,

00:12:05.415 --> 00:12:08.001
